    "ls|LS16_S6_U_302": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *302\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2015\n \n \nKHADI AND VILLAGE INDUSTRIES COMMISSION\n \n \n*302.\n \n \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n \n \n \nSHRIMATI SAKUNTALA LAGURI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government is aware of pivotal role played by the Khadi and Village \nIndustries Commission (KVIC) in transforming rural economy of the country and if so, the \ndeta\nils thereof; \n \n(b)\n \nwhether a number of Khadi Bhandars/outlets of KVIC have closed down over the years \nand if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor including the number of Khadi Bhandars \nwhich existed in 1997 and those at present, State/ UT\n-\nwise; \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government has introduced any scheme for modernisation of Khadi \nBhandars/outlets in the country and if so, the details thereof along with the funds earmarked \nfor the purpose; \n \n(d)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to set up Khadi outlets in \nforeign countries also and if \nso, the details thereof, country\n-\nwise; and \n \n(e)\n \nthe steps taken for promotion and export of khadi products?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI KALRAJ MISHRA)\n \n \n(a)to(\ne):\n \nA statement is laid on the T\nable of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART\nS \n \n(a) to (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION No.*302 FOR ANSWER ON 21.12.2015\n \n \n \n \n(\ni\n)\n \nKhadi & Village Industries Commission (KVIC) was set up in 1956 for the \ndevelopment of Khadi\n \nand Village Industries and for matters connected therewith. KVIC  \nextends financial support for creation of employment opportunities and for marketing of \nproducts of Khadi and Village Industries (KVIs). There are 7050 outlets of Khadi \nInstitutions, 7 Depa\nrtmental Khadi Gramodyog Bhavans (KGBs) and 1 Gramshilpa in \nthe country.  Government has taken various steps to support Khadi and Village Industries \nInstitutions,  including programmes like Janshree Bima Yojana (JBY), Market Promotion \nDevelopment Assistanc\ne (MPDA) Scheme, Workshed Scheme for Khadi Artisans,  \nInterest Subsidy Eligibility Certificate (ISEC) Scheme for the development of Khadi \nsector.  Budget allocated to KVIC in the year 2015\n-\n16 is Rs. 231.72 crore.\n \n \n \n \n(\nii\n)\n \nThe performance of KVIC outlets for the \nlast three years are given below: \n \n \n \nYear\n \nSales including supply\n \nto Govt. \nDepartments \n \n(Rs. in lakhs)\n \n2012\n-\n13\n \nRs. 5425.90\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \nRs. 8705.20\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \nRs.12513.72\n \n \n(iii)\n \n4 Khadi Bhandars\n/outlets of KVIC located at Hyderabad (2000), Visakhapatnam \n(2000), Lucknow (2001)\n \nand Bangalore (2010) have closed down due to non\n-\nviability. \n \n \n(iv)\n \nAt the end of 1997\n-\n98 financial year, i.e. as on 31.03.1998, there were 27 \nBhawans/Vastragar/Bhandars of K\nVIC. At present,23 Bhawans/Vastragar/Bhandars of KVIC \n(7 Departmental Bhawans, 1 Gramshilpa and 15 Branches) are functional. The State wise list \nis given below: \n \n \nKGB \n(No.)\n \nState\n \nOutlets \n \n(No.)\n \nDepartmental Sales Outlets\n \n1\n \nDelhi\n \n \n1\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan\n, Regal Building, Connaught Circus, \nNew Delhi working at Gramshilpa, A\n-\n1, Baba Kharak Singh \nMarg, Connaught Circus, New Delhi\n \n2\n \nSector\n-\n8, Shop No:1, RK Puram, New Delhi\n-\n110022 \n \n3\n \nIIT Shopping Centre, Hauz\n \nKhas, New Delhi\n-\n110016 \n \n4\n \nF\n-\n52, MadhuVihar\n, Delhi\n-\n110092 \n \n5\n \nD\n-\n9A, Sector\n-\n27, NOIDA (U.P)\n \n6\n \nAshoka Hotel, Chanakyapuri, New Delhi\n-\n110021 \n \n7\n \nSector\n-\n3, Rohini, New Delhi\n \n2\n \n8\n \nGramshilpa, A\n-\n1, Baba Kharak Singh Marg, Connaught Circus, \nNew Delhi \n \n\n3\n \nMaharashtra\n \n9\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan\n, KVIC, Campus, Irla Road, Vile \nParle (W) Mumbai\n–\n \n400056\n \n10\n \nViral Apartment, S.V. Road, Mumbai. \n \n4\n \nGoa\n \n11\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan, KVIC, Katkar Building,Old Station \nRoad, Margaon, Goa\n \n12\n \nMunicipal Building, Dr. A.B Road, Panaji\n-\n403011 \n \n5\n \nBhopal\n \n13\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan, KVIC, Jawahar\n \nChowk, Bhopal\n \n14\n \n27, Bhadbada Road, T.T. Nagar, Bhopal \n-\n \n462 003\n \n6\n \nKerala\n \n15\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan, Pallimukku, M.G. Road, \nErnakulam, Cochin\n \n16\n \nKhadi Silk Emporium East Fort, Trivandrum \n-\n \n695 023\n \n17\n \nShop No.15, Vijayanthi Building, Guruvayoor\n-\n \n680 101\n \n7\n \nWest \nBengal\n \n18\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan, KVIC, Chitaranjan Avenue Kolkata\n \n19\n \nBijwai Chand Road, Baidyanath\n \nKatra, Burdwan\n-\n713101 \n \n20\n \n46/2/1A, Gariahat Road, Kolkata\n-\n700019 \n \n21\n \nThe Mall, \nDarjeeling \n-\n \n734 101\n \n22\n \nIIT, Kharaghpur\n \n8\n \nBihar\n \n23\n \nKhadi Gramodyog\n \nBhavan, ‘B’ Block, 1\nst\nFloor, Maurya\n \nlok\n \nComplex, Patna\n-\n \n800001\n \n \n(v) \n \nTo renovate the outlets and to strengthen the production infrastructure, Government \nhas a separate scheme called “Strengthening of Infrastructure of Existing Weak Khadi \nInstitutions and assistance for Marketing Infrastructure”. This scheme provides for  ca\npital \nexpenditure, working capital and renovation of sales outlets.  KVIC has extended financial \nsupport to the tune of Rs.390.99 lakh for renovation and modernization of 52 sales outlets \nsince inception.\n \n \n(vi)\n \nThere is no proposal\n \nfor setting up of \n \nKhadi\n \noutlets in foreign countries.\n \n \n(vii)\n \nKVIC assists Khadi and Village\n \nIndustries  Institutions both in production and in \nmarketing of their products.  Export activities are encouraged through participation in Trade \nFairs including India International Trade \nFair (IITF) held every year at Delhi by India Trade \nPromotion Organisation (ITPO).  In addition, buyer\n-\nseller meets are also organized. KVIC \nhas been declared as deemed Export Promotion Council (EPC) for Khadi products in \nDecember 2006\n-\n1018 Khadi and Villa\nge Industries Institutions and PMEGP units have taken \nits membership to enter the export market. Government has introduced Interest Equalization \nScheme 2015 to boost exports in the sectors of handicrafts, agri\n-\nproducts, food processing \nand SMEs.  Governmen\nt is also implementing the International Cooperation Scheme to \nenable exporters to participate in International Trade Fairs and Exhibitions.\n \n \n \n***\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_303": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †303\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015\n†303. AWARENESS CAMPAIGN AGAINST NARCOTIC DRUGS\nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:\nSHRI ARJUN MEGHWAL:\nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to launch large scale awareness campaign against\nabuse of narcotic drugs in the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UTwise;\n(c) whether the Government is aware of the work being done by Rashtriya Samaj Raksha\nSansthan in this field; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government to streamline its functioning\nfor effective implementation across the country?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI VIJAY SAMPLA)\n(a) & (b): The Ministry already conducts following awareness generation programmes aginsts\nDrug abuse:\ni) Sensitization and preventive education programmes in collaboration with National Institute of\nSocial Defence (NISD) in schools and colleges on regular basis.\nii) Awareness and outreach programmes involving organizations like Nehru Yuva Kendra\nSangathan (NYKS), National Service Scheme (NSS) etc. Due to high r ate of prevalence of\naddiction in Punjab, the Ministry has assigned, in October 2014, an awareness generation\nprogramme in the State through NYKS. The Ministry organized two Regional Workshops\nduring year 2015-16 in collaboration with NSS at Shillong and I ndore to sensitize students. The\nCo-ordinators of NSS from all the North Eastern States, Odisha and West Bengal attended the\nWorkshop at Shillong and NSS Co-ordinators from Madhya Pradesh, Chhatisgarh and Rajashtan\nattended the Workshop at Indore. The Programme was also attended by a large number of\nstudents.\niii) The Ministry also uses print, electronic and social media for creating awareness. Information\nregarding ill -effects of alcoholism and drug abuse is also d isseminated in regional languages\n\n�������� ���� ���� ������ ������ ���������� ���������� ������ ������� ��� ������� ���� ����� �������\nadvertisement in news papers.\niv) The Ministry celebrates at International Day against Drug Abuse and Illicit Trafficking on\n26th June every year by holding functions and organizing exhibitions to sensitize the people\nabout the ill effects of drug abuse. National Awards are also conferred to individuals and\ninstitutions in order to recognise the efforts and encourage excellence in the fi eld of prevention\nof substance abuse.\nv) Ministry has set up a National Toll Free drug de -addiction helpline Number 1800 -11-0031\nw.e.f. 07.01.2015 to help the victims of Drug abuse, their family and society at large.\n(c) & (d): Yes, Madam. The National Institute of Social Defence (NISD) is the Nodal Institute\nfor training and research in the field of social defence. The Institute is mainly assigned the task\nof human resource development in the areas of substance abuse prevention, care of older persons\nand o ther social defence issues including beggary prevention. NISD undertakes following\nactivities in the field of drug abuse:\n(i) Organizes sensitization programme in Schools and Colleges and skill development\nprogramme for its target group in collaboration wi th Regional Resource and Training Centre s\n(RRTC) and other collaborating partners.\n(ii) Conducts regular Consultative Meeting with functionaries of RRTCs for effective\nimplementation of Sensitization and Skill Development Programmes.\n(iii) Participates in various fairs like India International Trade Fair, Shilpotsav at Delhi Haat  etc.\nby placing stalls for dissemination of information on drug abuse.\n(iv) In order to strengthen the infrastructure and manpower of NISD for streamlining its\nfunctioning, all formalities for construction of its own office building have been completed.\n*****",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_304": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 304 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2015 \nTeachers' Education \n \n†*304. DR. KRISHAN PRATAP: \n  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has reviewed the T eachers' training and education from time to \ntime and if so, the details of such reviews along with the corrective actions taken thereon; \n \n(b)  whether the Justice Verma Commission recommended the revamping of Teachers' \ntraining and education in the country; \n \n(c)  the salient features of  the observations/recommendations made by the Commission; and \n \n(d)  the follow-up action taken thereon along w ith the steps taken by the Government to \nprovide the quality on-the-job training to the teachers in the country? \n \n  \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n \n \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (d) of THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 304 FOR REPLY ON 21.12.2015 ASKED BY DR. KRISHAN \nPRATAP REGARDING TEACHERS’ EDUCATION  \n(a) to (d) The National Council for Teacher Education (NCTE), a Statutory Body set up under \nthe NCTE Act, 1993  is mandated to achieve pl anned and coordinated development of the \nTeacher Education system in the country and for determination and maintenance of standards for \nTeacher Education. The NCTE lays down Regula tions and Norms and Standards for conduct of \nTeacher Education programmes.  These Norms and Standards and Regulations of the NCTE are \nreviewed and revised from time to time. \nThe functioning of NCTE has been reviewed by various Committees in the past.  To \neffectively deal with the issues in regulati ng Teacher Education and to handle new challenges \nthrown up by the fast changing sc enario in the field of educa tion, the Ministry of Human \nResource Development had set up a four Member Committee under the Chairmanship of Shri \nP.K. Kaul, Former Cabinet Secretary, in Febr uary, 2002 to study the wo rking of the NCTE and \nmake recommendations on streamlining its activiti es with a view of ensuring its effective \nfunctioning. \nFurther, on the directions of Hon’ble Supreme Court, a High Powered Commission under \nJustice J.S. Verma was set up in the year 2011 to examine the t eacher education sector in the \ncountry.  The Justice Verma Commission subm itted its report in August 2012. The JVC has \ngiven a set of 30 recommendations to improve th e teacher education sector.  Some of the \nrecommendations of the JVC inter alia included; the need to anchor t eacher education programs \nin composite institutions, first degree to be in face to face mode only, the need on enhancement \nof investment in teacher education, enhancemen t of duration of M.Ed programme, strengthening \nof Teacher Education Institutions, regulatory reform in the NCTE and so on. \nIn the light of these recommendations, the NC TE has revised its Regulations and notified \nthe NCTE Regulations in November 2014. \nThe Central Government also implements the Centrally Sponsored Scheme on Teacher \nEducation (CSSTE) through which State Governme nts/UTs are financially supported to bring \nabout institutional and infrastructural developm ent in their Teacher Education Institutions \n\n(TEIs).The CSSTE was reviewed  by National Council of Educa tional Research and Training \n(NCERT) in August, 2009.  Thereafter, the CSSTE was restructured and the revised scheme was \nlaunched in June, 2012. The budget allocation under the CSSTE for 2015-16 is Rs. 557.60 crore.   \nFurther, under the Sarva Shiksha Abhiya n (SSA) and Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha \nAbhiyan (RMSA), flagship programmes of the Ce ntral Government to improve elementary and \nsecondary education in the countr y, there is a provision for in -service training of teachers for \ntheir professional development.  The SSA sup ports 30 days of induction training for newly \nrecruited teachers and there is a provision for 20 days of annual training for in-service teachers. \nSimilarly, under RMSA, there is a provision for 5 da ys of in-service trai ning of all secondary \nteachers, Principals and Vice-Principals.  Ther e is also a provision for 10 days of induction \ntraining for all newly recruited teachers. \nIn December, 2014 the Central Government has launched the Pandit Madan Mohan \nMalviya National Mission on T eachers and Teaching (PMMMNMTT) programme which is \naimed at holistic and comprehensive professional development of teachers and teacher educators.  \nThe CSSTE is being run as a sub-programme under the umbrella of the PMMMNMTT.  \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_305": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.305 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015 \n \nImpact of Climate Change in Himalayas \n \n 305. SHRI THUPSTAN CHHEWANG:  \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has developed a system  of monitoring the im pact of recession of \nGlaciers in the Himalayas on a regular basis; \n(b) if so, the institution entrusted with this task and the data collected during the last ten \nyears; \n(c) whether the institution so designated ha s the capability in terms of manpower, \nequipments and technology etc. to efficiently carry out the task assigned; \n(d) whether the Government has formulated any comprehensive Action Plan to combat the \nimpact of climate change in the Himalayas; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHAR GE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (c) Under the Himalayan Glaciological  Programme sponsored by the Department of \nScience and Technology, Government of Indi a, studies related to  mass balance, hydro-\nmeteorology, glacier dynamics, glacier length/area changes and climate studies of the Himalayan \nglaciers are being carried out.  A Centre for Glaciology has been established at Wadia Institute of \nHimalayan Geology, Dehradun. The Centre has init iated a coordinated research initiative on \nHimalayan glaciology to understand the effects of climate change on the gl aciers in order to \ndevelop strategies for climate change adaptability for sustaining Himalayan Eco-system. \n \n Further, in a collaborative project with Mi nistry of Environment, Forest and Climate \nChange,  lndian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) has carried out mapping of Himalayan \nglaciers covering lndus, Ganga and Brahmaputra basins using lndian satellite data during 2004 to \n2011. The study shows that there are 34,919 glaciers spread over 75,779 sq. km. glaciated area in \nthe entire Himalayas. Further,  ISRO has monitored the glaci er advance and retreat of 2018 \nglaciers, across the Himalayan region using satellite data of 2000-2001 and 2010-2011.  \n \n\n(d) & (e) The National Mission for Sustai ning the Himalayan Ecosystem (NMSHE) under \nNational Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC)  aims at evolving conservation measures for \nsustaining and safeguarding the Himalayan glaciers and mountain ecosystems through \nestablishment of monitoring network, promo tion of community based management, human \nresource development, and strengthening regiona l cooperation. Major in itiatives taken under \nNMSHE include creation of four Thematic Task  Forces on Himalayan Agriculture, Traditional \nKnowledge systems, Forest Resources and Plant Diversity, and Fauna and Wildlife Habitats. It \nalso includes networking of related institutions  for focused research in different themes of \nHimalayan ecosystem. Programmes have been init iated for training in th e field of glaciology, \nawareness programmes for community  based organizations and offi cials related to the Indian \nHimalayan Region. State Climate Change Centres have been set up in the seven Himalayan \nstates, namely, Jammu and Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Manipur, Mizoram, Tripura, Sikkim and \nMeghalaya. \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_306": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 306 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015   \n \n GM Crops \n \n306.   SHRI DHARAM VIRA:   \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORE STS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is planning to permit Genetically Modified Mustard crop to be \ncultivated across the country;  \n(b)  if so, the details in this regard and the re asons therefore along with the details of the data \nand information related to its safety tests; and  \n(c)  the measures being taken by the Governme nt to implement the recommendations of the \nSupreme Court appointed Technical Comm ittee and the Parliamentary Standing \nCommittee on Agriculture on any open air release of Genetic ally Modified Organisms \nincluding field trials and cultivation? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHAR GE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) & (b):   The Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC) notified under Rules, 1989 is \nthe apex body to accord approval for large scal e use and commercial release of genetically \nmodified organisms. GEAC had approved the application from Centre for the Genetic \nManipulation of Crop Plants, University of De lhi (CGMCP) for permission to conduct BRL-II \ntrials of transgenic mustard hybr id (DMH-11) containing bar, barn ase and barstar genes in three \nplaces i.e. Ludhiana, Bhatinda and Delhi. Th e CGMCP has submitted the application to the \nGEAC for consideration for commer cial release of transgenic mu stard. Government of India has \na policy of taking a view on GM crop only after full scientific valuation of its biosafety and \nimpact on the environment and on the consumers. Introduction of any new GM crop is preceded \nby a careful analysis of risks a nd evaluation of long te rm benefits for whic h extensive rules and \nguidelines framed by the Government for evaluating environmental and health safety impacts of \ngenetically modified organisms.  \n  \n(c)  The Technical Expert Committee (TEC) appointed by Supreme Court has submitted two \nreports; one report having the concurrence of  the 5 Member TEC and the second report \nsubmitted by the 6\nth Member.  Both the reports have high lighted certain regulatory gaps and \nsuggestions for improving the Biosafety Regula tion in India.  Five members of TEC have \nrecommended that the GM crop field trials be put on hold till all recommendations are \n\nimplemented. Dr. Paroda, the 6th member recommended that the existing safety norms prescribed \nby the GEAC for conduct of field trials are adeq uate.  The Union of India (UoI) has not accepted \nthe recommendations for imposing moratorium on GM field trials on the grounds that include (i) \nthe current regulations and Protocols followed in  biosafety assessment are as per international \nbest practices, (ii) field trials ar e an integral part of biosafety assessment and trials are necessary \nto generate biosafety data, (iii ) any ban on GM crop fiel d trials would stop al l research activities \nin the country which may have a long term imp lications on food security issues. The UoI had \nfiled its objection in the Supreme Court th rough a joint Common Affidavit on 1.4.2014.  The \nUOI is of the view that research in GM and c onfined field trials for generating biosafety data \nwith all due precautions should be allowed to continue in the natio nal interest. As of date, the \nSupreme Court has not imposed any ban on GM crop field trials.  \n \nThe recommendations made by the Parliamentary Standing Committee on Agriculture on \nCultivation of Genetically Modified F ood Crops – Prospects and Effects (37\nth report) for \nstopping of all field trials of GM  crops were not agreed to and it was informed that it is not \nadvisable to stop the field trials of transgenic crops as this would deny access of Indian farmers \nto such technology and since the biosafety regulatory mechanism for GM crops in India is of \nglobal standards.   \n*** ",
