    "ls|LS16_S6_U_453": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 453 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1.12.2015 \n \nProposals for Ecologically Sensitive Areas \n \n \n453. KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE  \n \n Will the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether only Five States have submitted proposals for Ec ologically Sensitive Areas \n (ESAs) on the Western Ghats Notificat ion and if so, the detail thereof; \n(b)  whether some States have sought more ti me for submitting their proposals and if so, \n the details thereof; and \n(c) the action taken by the Government thereon? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n  \n(a), (b) & (c) The Ministry has i ssued a fresh notification dated 4 th September, 2015 in \nsupersession of the earlier dr aft notification issued on 10 th March, 2014 declaring \necologically sensitive area in the Western Ghats. The states of Gujarat, Maharashtra, Goa, \nKarnataka and Kerala have submitted thei r proposals to the Government demarcating \necologically sensitive areas (ESAs) in the We stern Ghats of their States by physical \nverification. The Government of Tamil Nadu ha s sought additional time for submitting their \nproposal. \n    \n********** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_454": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  454 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1ST  DECEMBER, 2015/AGRAHAYANA 10, 1937 (SAKA) \n \nACCIDENTAL DEATH AND SUICIDE \n  \n454. SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether according to the National  Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) data  \nregarding accidental death and suicides in  India released recently, the number \nof suicides, which has declined since 2011 has  increased marginally to 1.3 lakh \nin 2014; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the southern States  including Kerala and Tamil Nadu have  twice \nthe national suicide rate in proportion to their population; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARIBHAI PARATHIBHAI CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d) :  As per data available with National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) , \na total of 135585, 135445,  134799 and 131666 suicides were reported during \n2011, 2012, 2013 and 2014 respectively, showing a declining trend. The \nState/UT wise number of suicides and rate of suicides during 2014 is at \nAnnexure. As against the  national average suicide rate of 10.6 the suicide rate \nreported in Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu and Telangana are \n12.0, 17.8, 23.9, 23.4 and 26.5 respectively for the year 2014. \n***** \n\n1Page of 1 Page of Annexure-I \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 454 FOR 01.12.2015 \n \nState/UT wise number of suicides and rate of suicides during 2011-2014 \nSl. \nNo. State/UT \n2011 2012 2013 2014 \nNumber \nof \nSuicides \nRate of \nSuicides  \nNumber \nof \nSuicides \nRate of \nSuicides  \nNumber \nof \nSuicides \nRate of \nSuicides  \nNumber \nof \nSuicides \nRate of \nSuicides  \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 15077 18 14238 16.6 14607 16.9 6101 12 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 134 10 130 10.3 229 18 156 12.1 \n3 ASSAM 2726 9 3264 10.5 3553 11.3 3546 11.1 \n4 BIHAR 795 1 759 0.8 1057 1.1 719 0.7 \n5 CHHATTISGARH 6756 27 5654 22.9 5436 21.7 5683 22.4 \n6 GOA 293 20 289 15.8 328 17.4 291 15.1 \n7 GUJARAT 6382 11 7110 11.8 7166 11.8 7225 11.7 \n8 HARYANA 3245 13 2827 10.9 3316 12.6 3203 11.9 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 443 7 528 7.7 554 8 644 9.2 \n10 JAMMU & KASHMIR 287 2 414 3.5 302 2.5 258 2.1 \n11 JHARKHAND 1212 4 1319 4.1 1460 4.5 1300 4 \n12 KARNATAKA 12622 21 12753 21.2 11266 18.5 10945 17.8 \n13 KERALA 8431 25 8490 24.3 8646 24.6 8446 23.9 \n14 MADHYA PRADESH 9259 13 9775 13.3 9446 12.6 9039 11.9 \n15 MAHARASHTRA 15947 14 16112 14 16622 14.3 16307 13.9 \n16 MANIPUR 33 1 41 1.6 37 1.5 50 2 \n17 MEGHALAYA 153 5 128 4.8 137 5.1 99 3.6 \n18 MIZORAM 90 8 173 17 95 9.2 116 11.1 \n19 NAGALAND 33 2 30 1.3 37 1.6 13 0.6 \n20 ODISHA 5241 13 5027 12.2 5252 12.6 4160 9.9 \n21 PUNJAB 966 4 1030 3.7 972 3.4 943 3.3 \n22 RAJASTHAN 4348 6 4821 7 4860 6.9 4459 6.3 \n23 SIKKIM 184 30 181 29.1 184 29.3 244 38.4 \n24 TAMIL NADU 15963 22 16927 24.9 16601 24.3 16122 23.4 \n  TELANGANA - - - - - - 9623 26.5 \n25 TRIPURA 703 19 844 23 961 25.9 762 20.3 \n26 UTTAR PRADESH 4843 2 4422 2.2 5286 2.5 3590 1.7 \n27 UTTARAKHAND 317 3 424 4.2 365 3.6 207 2 \n28 WEST BENGAL 16492 18 14957 16.5 13055 14.3 14310 15.5 \n  TOTAL (STATES) 132975 11 132667 11.1 131830 10.9 128561 10.5 \n  UNION TERRITORIES:                 \n29 A & N ISLANDS 136 36 121 23.6 151 28.8 155 28.9 \n30 CHANDIGARH 105 10 114 7.4 97 6 105 6.3 \n31 D & N HAVELI 63 18 66 17.6 84 21.4 76 18.7 \n32 DAMAN & DIU 33 14 36 12.6 29 9.8 27 8.7 \n33 DELHI (UT) 1716 10 1899 9.9 2059 10.4 2095 10.3 \n34 LAKSHADWEEP 0 0 1 1.3 3 3.9 3 3.8 \n35 PUDUCHERRY 557 45 541 36.8 546 35.6 644 40.4 \n  TOTAL (UTS) 2610 13 2778 11.9 2969 12.3 3105 12.5 \n  TOTAL (ALL–INDIA) 135585 11 135445 11.2 134799 11 131666 10.6 \nSource:  Accidental Deaths & Suicides in India \n    \n******* \n     \n\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_455": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 455. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1ST  DECEMBER, 2015/AGRAHAYANA 10, 1937 (SAKA) \n \nPAROLE TO PRISONERS \n  \n455.  SHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any  proposal to formulate a universal \npolicy that covers the entire country and is  applicable to all States so that \nthere are unified existing rules and guidelines laid down for granting parole \nto prisoners convicted of heinous crimes including anti-national activities; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the States enjoy vast discretionary powers that could lead to \nmisuse in granting parole to prisoners and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) whether the Go vernment is aware  of any complaints of violation of \nguidelines by the States in order to provide  preferential treatment in \ngranting of parole to prisoners and if so, the action taken in this regard? \n  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARIBHAI PARATHIBHAI CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d):  No, Madam. “Prisons” is a State subject as per entry 4 of List II \nof the Seventh Schedule to the Constitution of India. Therefore, the \nadministration and management of prisons is primarily the res ponsibility of \nthe State Governments. The grant of parole (leave) is an executive \nfunction, hence exercised by the respective Stat es/UTs. The parole is \ngranted by the respective States/UTs as per their existing Jail manuals and \nrules notified under their A cts and are decided on merits of each case as \nper laid down norms. \n***** \n\n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_456": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 456 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015 \n \nUse of Polythene Bags \n \n456.  SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI:  \nSHRIMATI SANTOSH AHLAWAT: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORE STS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the details of the policy of the Government in regard to th e use of Polythene Plastic carry \n bags in the country;  \n(b)  whether Polythene Plastic carry bags remain in use despite the imposition of a ban;  \n(c) if so, whether any monitoring of the same  is undertaken at the Ministerial level;  \n(d)  if so, the details and the type of  monitoring undertaken in this regard;  \n(e) whether the Government has any proposal to implement a uniform law across the country \n with regard to a ban on the use of Polythene Plastic carry bags;  \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(g)  the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Cl imate Change has notified the Plastic Waste \n(Management and Handling) Rules, 2011, which prohi bit manufacture, sale, stocking and use of \nplastic carry bags of less than 40 micron in th ickness. The pigments and colourants used for \nmanufacturing of plastic carry ba gs should conform to the Bureau of Indian Standard.  No carry \nbags should be made available free of cost by  retailers to consumers. The name, registration \nnumber of the manufacturer and thickness shou ld be printed on each plastic carry bag. The \nmunicipal authorities are required to notify the minimum price for carry bags so as to minimize \nplastic waste generation. Any pers on manufacturing carry bags or engage in recycling of plastic \nwaste is required to register with concerned State Pollution C ontrol Board or Pollution Control \nCommittee.   \n \n (b)   The use of polythene bags above 40 micron thickness is permitted as per the Plastic Waste \n(Management and Handling) Rules, 2011, and are in use. \n \n\n(c) & (d) Does not arise in view of the reply to part (b) above. However, as per, Plastic Waste          \n(Management and Handling) Rules, 2011, the State Pollution Control Boards is responsible for \nenforcement of provisions of the abovementioned rules in respect of manufacture, registration \nand recycling. The Municipal authority is respon sible for enforcement of provisions relating to \nuse and sale of such bags in th eir jurisdiction.   As per the m echanism under the Rules, all State \nPollution Control Boards (SPCBs) and the Pollu tion Control Committees (PCCs) are required to \nsubmit annual report to the Central Pollution Cont rol Board (CPCB), which in turn is required \nprepare a consolidated report on use and manageme nt of plastic waste and forward it to this \nMinistry.   \n \n(e), (f) & (g) There is no proposal at present to impose a ban on the use of plastic carry bags.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_457": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †457\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015\n†457. BEGGARY\nSHRIMATI NEELAM SONKER:\nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the total number of beggars in the country, State-wise;\n(b) the number of physically fit, disabled and drug addicted beggars, out of the total number of\nbeggars;\n(c) whether any gang of beggars is operating in metro cities forcing small children into begging\nthrough kidnapping them or amputation of their limbs and if so, the details thereof and the\nreaction of the Government thereto;\n(d) whether the Government has initiated any action to ascertain the reasons for the increasing\nnumber of beggars in the country and to check this tendency and if so, the details thereof;\n(e) whether the Government has formulated any policy to bring physically fit beggars into\nmainstream; and\n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI VIJAY SAMPLA)\n(a) & (b):  As per the State -wise Census  2011 details furnished by the Office of Registrar\nGeneral & Census Commissioner, India, the number of Beggars, Vagrants etc. in India as\nfollows:\ni. Total Beggars, Vagrants etc. -413670\nii. Total Disabled Beggars, Vagrants etc.- 70506\nThe State-wise details are enclosed as Annexure-I and Annexure-II respectively.\nNo authentic data about Drug Addicted Beggars is available in the Ministry.\n(c): No such information is available in the Ministry.\n(d) to (f): The States are responsible for taking necessary preventive and rehabilitative steps. As\nper available information, 20 States and 2 Union Territories have either enacted their own Anti -\nBeggary Legislation or adopted legislations enacted by other States/UTs. Presently, there is no\nscheme of the Central Government on Beggary. National Consultation Meeting were held with\nthe representatives of Central/State Governments, NGOs, Institutes and Individual Experts in the\nfield of Beggary to discuss the issues related to Beggary. In these meetings, it was inter -alia,\nrecommended to explore the feasibility of framing a Central Scheme for rehabilitation of\nBeggars.\n*****\n\nANNEX-I\nTotal Beggars, Vagrants etc : Census-2011\nIndia/State/Ut Persons Males Females\n1 2 3 4\nINDIA 413670 221673 191997\nJAMMU & KASHMIR 4134 2550 1584\nHIMACHAL PRADESH 809 504 305\nPUNJAB 7939 5197 2742\nCHANDIGARH 121 87 34\nUTTARAKHAND 3320 2374 946\nHARYANA 8682 6504 2178\nNCT OF DELHI 2187 1343 844\nRAJASTHAN 25853 15271 10582\nUTTAR PRADESH 65835 41859 23976\nBIHAR 29723 14842 14881\nSIKKIM 68 46 22\nARUNACHAL PRADESH 114 59 55\nNAGALAND 124 65 59\nMANIPUR 263 117 146\nMIZORAM 53 33 20\nTRIPURA 1490 607 883\nMEGHALAYA 396 172 224\nASSAM 22116 7269 14847\nWEST BENGAL 81244 33086 48158\nJHARKHAND 10819 5522 5297\nODISHA 17965 9981 7984\nCHHATTISGARH 10198 4995 5203\nMADHYA PRADESH 28695 17506 11189\nGUJARAT 13445 8549 4896\nDAMAN & DIU 22 15 7\nDADRA & NAGAR HAVELI 19 7 12\nMAHARASHTRA 24307 14020 10287\nANDHRA PRADESH 30218 16264 13954\nKARNATAKA 12270 6436 5834\nGOA 247 131 116\nLAKSHADWEEP 2 0 2\nKERALA 4023 2397 1626\nTAMIL NADU 6814 3789 3025\nPUDUCHERRY 99 54 45\nA & N ISLANDS 56 22 34\n\nANNEX-II\nTotal Disabled Beggars, Vagrants etc. : Census 2011\nAea Name Persons Male Female\n2 3 4\nINDIA 70506 43141 27365\nState - JAMMU & KASHMIR (01) 834 603 231\nState - HIMACHAL PRADESH (02) 148 112 36\nState - PUNJAB (03) 1296 825 471\nState - CHANDIGARH (04) 33 28 5\nState - UTTARAKHAND (05) 551 385 166\nState - HARYANA (06) 825 606 219\nState - NCT OF DELHI (07) 588 407 181\nState - RAJASTHAN (08) 2331 1588 743\nState - UTTAR PRADESH (09) 6003 4220 1783\nState - BIHAR (10) 5276 3399 1877\nState - SIKKIM (11) 39 22 17\nState - ARUNACHAL PRADESH (12) 10 7 3\nState - NAGALAND (13) 42 26 16\nState - MANIPUR (14) 69 44 25\nState - MIZORAM (15) 20 15 5\nState - TRIPURA (16) 147 78 69\nState - MEGHALAYA (17) 117 60 57\nState - ASSAM (18) 5311 2884 2427\nState - WEST BENGAL (19) 13798 7697 6101\nState - JHARKHAND (20) 3224 1849 1375\nState - ODISHA (21) 6550 3850 2700\nState - CHHATTISGARH (22) 2537 1329 1208\nState - MADHYA PRADESH (23) 4868 3135 1733\nState - GUJARAT (24) 1686 1179 507\nState - DAMAN & DIU (25) 5 5 0\nState - DADRA & NAGAR HAVELI (26) 5 2 3\nState - MAHARASHTRA (27) 4456 2738 1718\nState - ANDHRA PRADESH (28) 5593 3343 2250\nState - KARNATAKA (29) 1891 1178 713\nState - GOA (30) 39 24 15\nState - LAKSHADWEEP (31) 0 0 0\nState - KERALA (32) 945 665 280\nState - TAMIL NADU (33) 1239 821 418\nState - PUDUCHERRY (34) 23 11 12\nState - ANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS (35) 7 6 1",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_458": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 458 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1ST  DECEMBER, 2015/AGRAHAYANA 10, 1937 (SAKA) \n \nBOREWELL RELATED CASES \n  \n458. SHRI G. HARI: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that borewells  are continuing to be death traps in \nthe country; \n \n(b) the total number of cases related to  borewells reported during each of \nthe last three years and the current year, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps to make it mandatory to \nclose down the borewells which are not in use or  keep them properly \nblocked; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARIBHAI PARATHIBHAI CHAUDHARY) \n \n \n(a) to (b): National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) has started collecting \ndata on cases reported and   persons injured & died due to fall into bore well \nsince 2014.   Hence no comparison can be made on increase/decrease in \ndeaths due to fall in bore well. A total  of 50 Persons died due to fall into bore \nwell in the country during 2014. State/UT wise cases reported and persons \ndied due to fall in bore well during 2014 is enclosed at Annexure-I. \n \n(c) to (d): The Government is aware of occurrence of such incidents of small \nchildren falling into open bore wells. The Hon’ble Supreme Court has directed \nall the States & UTs through Orders dated 11th February, 2010 and 6th \nAugust, 2010, to take preventive actions on cases of children falling in bore \nwells as per Annexure-II.    \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.Q.No. 458 for 01.12.2015 \n \nThe State Governments of Andhra Pradesh, Assam, Himachal Pradesh, \nJammu & Kashmir, Karnataka, Kerala, Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, \nRajasthan, Orissa, Tamil Nadu, Uttar Pradesh, Haryana, and West Bengal \nhave issued instructions to the concerned agencies to adopt safety \nmeasures such as the closure of abandoned wells after filling and also \nmaking of provision to cap them . Further, the landowners before taking up \nany construction works must inform the concerned authorities. Further, the \nMinistry of Water Resources had taken steps for preventing such fatal \naccidents; look into the legal and statutory measures available, action \nrequired for preventing such accidents and to suggest other administrative \nand technical measures for prevention of such accidents.  \n \nFurther, ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India and therefore, the State Governments \nare primarily responsible for prevention, detection, registra tion and \ninvestigation of crime and for prosecuting the criminals through the \nmachinery of their law enforcement agencies as also for protecting the life \nand property of the citizens. \n******* \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n...3/- \n  \n\n-3- \n1page of 1 page of Annexure-I \nLS.U.Q.No. 458 for 01.12.2015 \n \n \nNumber of Cases, Persons Injured& Died by Fall into Bore Well during 2014 \n \nSl. \nNo. State/UT No. of  \nCases \nFalls(into Borewell) \nNo. of Persons Injured  No. of Persons Died \nMale Female \nTrans- \ngender Total Male Female \nTrans- \ngender Total \n STATES          \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n2 ARUNACHAL \nPRADESH 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n3 ASSAM 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n4 BIHAR 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n5 CHHATTISGARH 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n6 GOA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n7 GUJARAT 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n8 HARYANA 7 0 0 0 0 6 1 0 7 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 JAMMU & KASHMIR 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n11 JHARKHAND 1 0 0 0 0 2 1 0 3 \n12 KARNATAKA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n13 KERALA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n14 MADHYA PRADESH 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n15 MAHARASHTRA 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n16 MANIPUR 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n17 MEGHALAYA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n18 MIZORAM 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n19 NAGALAND 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n20 ODISHA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n21 PUNJAB 3 0 0 0 0 3 0 0 3 \n22 RAJASTHAN 9 0 0 0 0 5 4 0 9 \n23 SIKKIM 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n24 TAMIL NADU 8 0 0 0 0 7 1 0 8 \n25 TELANGANA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n26 TRIPURA 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n27 UTTAR PRADESH 14 1 0 0 1 13 1 0 14 \n28 UTTARAKHAND 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n29 WEST BENGAL 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n TOTAL (STATES) 47 1 0 0 1 41 8 0 49 \n UNIONTERRITORIES                   \n30 A & NISLANDS 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n31 CHANDIGARH 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n32 D & N HAVELI 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n33 DAMAN & DIU 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n34 DELHI (UT) 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n35 LAKSHADWEEP 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n36 PUDUCHERRY 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n TOTAL (UTs) 1 0 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 \n TOTAL (ALL INDIA) 48 1 0 0 1 42 8 0 50 \n\n-4- \n1page of 1 page of Annexure-II \nLS.U.Q.No. 458 for 01.12.2015 \n \n \nSafety measures/guidelines  as given in the Order dated 11.02.2010 of \nHon’ble Supreme Court are to be observed  by all the States:- \n \n(i)        “The owner of the land/premises, before taking any steps for \nconstruction bore well/tube well must inform in writing at least 15 \ndays in advance to the concerned authorities in the area, i.e., \nDistrict Collector /District Magistrate/Sarpanch of the Gram \nPanchayat/ Concerned officers of the Department of Ground Water/ \nPublic Health/Municipal Corporation, as the case may be, about the \nconstruction of bore well/tube well.  \n \n(ii)        Registration of all the drillin g agencies, viz., Govt./Semi Govt./ \nPrivate etc. should be mandatory with the district administration.  \n \n(iii)       Erection of signboard at the time of construction near the well \nwith the following details:-  \n \n(a) Complete address of the drilling agency at the time of \nconstruction/rehabilitation of well.  \n \n(b) Complete address of the user agency/owner of the well.  \n \n(iv)     Erection of barbed wire fencing or any other suitable barrier \naround the well during construction.  \n \n(v)       Construction of cement/concrete platform measuring \n0.50x0.50x0.60 meter (0.30 meter above ground level and 0.30 \nmeter below ground level) around the well casing.  \n \n(vi)     Capping of well assembly by welding steel plate or by providing a \nstrong cap to be fixed to the casing pipe with bolts and nutes.  \n \n(vii)     In case of pump repair, the tube well should not be left uncovered.  \n \n(viii)   Filling of mud pits and channels after completion of works.  \n \n(ix)     Filling up abandoned borewells by cla y/sand/boulders/pebbles/drill \ncuttings etc. from bottom to ground level.  \n...5/- \n\n-5- \nLS.U.Q.No. 458 for 01.12.2015 \n \n(x)      On completion of the drilling operations at a particular location, \nthe ground conditions are to be restored as before the start of \ndrilling.  \n \n(xi)     District Collector should be empowered to verify that the above \nguidelines are being followed a nd proper monitoring check about \nthe status of boreholes/tubewells are being taken care through the \nconcerned State/Central Government agencies.  \n \n(xii)      District/Block/Village wise status of bore wells/tubewells drilled \nviz. No. of wells in use, No. o f abandoned bore wells/tube wells \nfound open, No. of abandoned borewells/tube wells properly filled \nup to ground level and balance number of abandoned \nborewells/tubewells to be  filled up to ground level is to be \nmaintained at District Level. In rural area s, the monitoring of the \nabove is to be done through village Sarpanch and the Executive \nfrom the Agriculture Department.  \n \n(xiii)    If a borewell/tubewell is ‘Abandoned’ at any stage, a certificate \nfrom the concerned department of Ground Water/Public \nhealth/Municipal Corporation/Private contractor etc. must be \nobtained by the aforesaid agencies that the ‘Abandoned’ \nborewell/tubewell is properly filled upto the ground level.  Random \ninspection of the abandoned wells is also to be done by the \nExecutive of th e concern agency/department.  Information on all \nsuch data on the above are to be maintained in the District \nCollector/Block Development Office of the State.  \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_459": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 459 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015 \n \nEmployment and Rewards in Sports \n \n459. SHRI BAIJAYANT JAY PANDA:  \n         SHRI OM BIRLA \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to afford gainful employment \nopportunities to retired sportspersons within the sports sector;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government plans to extend the sports pension \nscheme to the sportspersons who have represented the nation at \ninternational or national level events; and  \n \n(d)  the number of land and such rewards announced by the centre \nalong with the number of their recipients during the last three years, \nState-wise, sports-wise and year-wise? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of You th Affairs & Sports provides monthly pension \nto meritorious sportspersons and financial assistance to  retired/active \nsportspersons who are living in indigent conditions. \n \n Recruitment of meritorious sports persons against vacancies \nreserved for sports quota is done in Central Government offices in terms \nof extant instructions of the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and \nPension. \n In terms of existing instructions of the Government, upto 5% of \ndirect recruit vacancies in Group ‘C’ and erstwhile Group ‘D’ are reserved \nfor meritorious sportspersons in Central Government offices. \n\n \n Further, Ministries/Department of Government of India can recruit \nmeritorious sportspersons  in any year in relaxation of the recruitment \nprocedure, to the extent that these, including all other reservations under \nexisting orders, do not exceed 50% of the total number of vacancies \nproposed to be filled by direct recruitment. Central Government \norganizations and Public Sector undertakings recruit meritorious \nsportspersons including medal winners in international sports events from \ntime to time for which they advertise in Employment News and other \nnewspapers. \n \n(c) No, Madam.  \n \n(d) The Government does not provide rewards like land to Sportspersons.  \n \n******* \n  \n\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_460": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.460 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015 \n \nRestrictions in Coastal Regulation Zone \n \n \n460.  PROF. SAUGATA ROY: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORE STS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to rela x the existing restriction in the Coastal \n Regulation Zone; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has launched any pilot project to protect the bio-diversity \n conservation through the involvement of vari ous stakeholders including general public \n and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  the visions of the National Biodiversity Aut hority for the bio-diversity conservation in the \n country? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHAR GE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) & (b) The Ministry has received representations from the coastal states for amendments \nin the Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) Notifi cation, 2011. A committee headed by Shri Shailesh \nNaik, Secretary, Ministry of Earth Sciences was constituted to examine and suggest amendments \nin the said Notification. The committee has recommended certain amendments therein.  \n \n(c) & (d) To meet the challenges of biodi versity conservation, the Ministry has taken \ncertain steps with the involvement  of various stakeholders, which inter-alia include, survey and \ninventorization of flora and fauna, assessment of forest cover,  establishment of Protected Area \nnetwork, conservation and community reserves, species oriented programmes etc.  \n \nThe Biological Diversity Act, 2002 aims at conserving biological resources in the country \nand regulation of access to ensure equitable shari ng of benefits arising out of their use. The \nNational Biodiversity Authority  and State Biodiversity Boar ds have been set up for \nimplementing provisions of the Act.  \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_461": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.461 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2ND DECEMBER, 2015 \n \nSETTING UP UNITS OF CHINESE COMPANIES \n \n461. SHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has cleared a proposal for setting up telecom \nmanufacturing units in the country including Tamil Nadu by Chinese companies; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether in view of the security concerns the Government has asked the \ncompany to reserve key technical positions for Indian nationals; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the company thereto? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) to (d)  Madam, information is being collected and will be laid on the table of the \nhouse as soon as possible.  \n \n******************* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_462": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 462 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, DECEMBER 02, 2015 \n \nWEATHER FORECASTING AGENCIES \n \n462.  ADV.  JOICE GEORGE: \n \n         Will the Minister of EARTH SCIENCES be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether private agencies are involved in weather forecasts in the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)   whether significant differences exist between the prediction of private \nagencies and Indian Meteorological Department and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c)  whether such agencies are prone to fudging data and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n(d)  whether the Government has any regulatory framework over such \nagencies and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES  \n(SHRI Y. S. CHOWDARY) \n \n(a) Yes Madam. Sky Met Weather Services is involved in weather forecasts \nin the country.  However, Earth System Science Organization –India \nMeteorological Department (ESSO -IMD) is the national agency for \nissuing long range forecast for the country.  \n(b) Government is aware that Sky Met Weather Services  has predicted \nseasonal rainfall of 102% of Long Period Average (LPA) for the 2015 \nsouthwest monsoon, whereas Earth System Science Organisation –\nIndia Meteorological Department (ESSO -IMD) has predicted monsoon \n2015 seasonal rainfall of 88% of LPA ±4% model error and the season \n(June-September) rainfall over the country as a whole was 86% of its  \nLPA. \n(c) Private Weather Services have no data network of their own. They \nwork on information available on public domain. Fudging of data is not \npossible.  \n(d) As of now, no regulatory framework exists over such agencies. \n \n****** \n \n  ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_463": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 463 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 02.12.2015 \n \n \nNATIONAL CYBER CRIME CENTER  \n \n463  SHRI P.R. SENTHIL NATHAN: \n                    \n Will the Minister of Communications & Information Technology be pleased to state: - \n \n(a) whether the Government has decided to set up a National Cyber Crime Centre; \n(b) if so, the details thereof;   \n(c) the purpose to set up National Cyber Crime Centre; and \n(d) the time by which the centre will start functioning? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY   \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) , (b) and (c): The Expert Group constituted in Ministry of Home Affairs to prepare a \nroadmap for effectively tackling cyber -crimes in the country has recommended to set up an \nIndian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) to fight against cyber crime in the country, \nwhich has been accepted by the Ministry of Home Affairs in-principle. \n \n(d) :    No timeline has since been fixed for starting the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination \nCentre (I4C). \n \n********* \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_464": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 464 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 02, 2015 \nAFFORDABLE HOUSING                                                         \n \nNo. 464.  DR. MANOJ RAJORIA:   \n   \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY \nALLEVIATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has received proposals for affordable \nhousing from corporates/developers and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the details of incentives, if any, the Government plans to extend to \ndevelopers/corporates for building affordable houses; \n(c) whether the Government intends to make some stringent rules for \ncapping commission of the mediators or property dealers in order to \nmake available affordable houses to common man; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN \nPOVERTY ALLEVIATION \n[SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO] \n \n \n(a): No, Madam.  Land and colonization being state subjects, state \nGovernments undertake affordable housing in partnership with \ncorporate/developers. \n \n(b): The Government of India, provides financial assistance to \nStates/UTs at the rate of Rs. 1.5 lakh per EWS house in an affordable  \nhousing project taken up under the Affordable Housing in Partnership  \nContd… \n\n-2- \n \n(AHP) vertical of “Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (PMAY) - Housing for \nAll (Urban)” Mission.  The other incentives being given include: \n \nI. Service tax exemptions for affordable housing projects under \nAffordable Housing in Partnership, JNNURM and RAY. \nII. 35AD, Deduction in respect of expenditure on specified \nbusiness under Income Tax, 1961. \nIII. Banks and Financial Institutions are encouraged to lend to \nhousing sector with Priority Sector Lending Norms, constitution \nof Credit Risk Guarantee Fund for low income housing, leverage \nto float bonds etc. \nIV. Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) allowed in construction and \ndevelopment sector with special exemptions for Affordable \nhousing. \nV. External Commercial Borrowing (ECB) has been allowed for \naffordable housing and slum improvement projects to enable \navailability of cheaper funds for low-cost housing. \n \n(c) & (d): No, Madam, there is no such proposal at this stage. \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_465": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS \n(DEPARTMENT OF PERSONNEL AND TRAINING) \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 465 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015) \n \nSTAFF STRENGTH OF CBI \n \n465. SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total sanctioned staff strength of  the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI)  from \nthe level of Constable to Director; \n \n(b)  whether several vacancies in almost  all categories exist in the CBI which is  adversely \naffecting its functioning; \n \n(c)  if so, the number of vacancies, category-wise and post-wise; \n \n(d)  the time since when these vacancies are lying vacant; and \n \n(e)  the steps taken or to be taken to fill up these vacancies? \n \nANSWER \n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions and Minister \nof State in the Prime Minister’s Office. (DR. JITENDRA SINGH) \n(a) to (c) :  The sanctioned staff strength in Executive ranks (from the level of Constable to \nDirector) in the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) is 5000 out of total sanctioned strength \nof 7274. A statement showing the number of vacancies category -wise and post -wise is \nenclosed at Annexure A.  The occurrence of vacancies and their filling up is an ong oing \nprocess. The number keeps on varying depending on the induction, promotion, retirement and \nrepatriation of the officers. With effective use and optimal deployment of existing personnel, \nthe vacancies have not affected the performance of the CBI adversely.  \n(d) & (e): The number of vacancies at various levels keeps changing from time to time. It is \nthe endeavor of Central Government as well as CBI to fill up vacant posts expeditiously.  To \nfill up the vacancies Government takes various steps as and when  necessary to ensure that \nvacancies are filled up speedily. Some of these steps are as under:- \n(i) Proposals for induction of officers at senior level in CBI  complete in all respects are \nplaced before the CBI Selection Committee for consideration without delay and on priority \nbasis. \n(ii) The proposal of CBI regarding relaxation in eligibility criteria for promotion of 149 \nSub Inspectors to Inspector has been approved.     \nContd….2/- \n \n\n \n--2-- \n(iii) UPSC has already recommended appointment of 80 Public Prosecutors in CBI, which \nhas been conveyed to CBI for further necessary action.  Approval has also been conveyed for \nextension in the deputation tenure of 31 Public Prosecutors on contract basis. \n(iv) Power has been delegated to Director, CBI for extending of deputation in respect of \nInspectors in CBI for the 9th and 10th year and also for extensions to 6th, 7th and 8th year which \nat present is approved by a Committee consisting of 3 members. \n(v) Approval has been conveyed to CBI for filling up of vacancies for Dy. SP through \nLimited Departmental Competitive Examinations (LDCE).    \n(viii) All the Recruitment Rules have already been revised as per requirement of CBI in \n2013. \n \n***** \n(vi) CBI has been exempted from consultation wit h UPSC for recruitment to the post of \nDSP for a period of 3 years.  \n(vii) Director Generals of Central Armed Police Forces and Chief Secretaries of State \nGovts. have been requested by CBI to spare officers of requisite seniority to join the CBI on \ndeputation basis.  Also, matter has been raised by Secretary (DOPT) in the meeting of State \nGeneral Administration Department/Personnel Secretaries held on 08.09.2015. \n\nAnnexure-A \nVACANCY POSITION AT A GLANCE- EXECUTIVE OFFICERS \nDesignation of posts Sanctioned \nStrength \nActual Strength Vacancy \nDirector 1 1 0 \nSpl. Directors/ Addl. Director 4 2 2 \nJoint Director 18 16 2 \nDy. Inspr. Genl. of Police 43 26 17 \nSr. Supdt. of Police 10 04 06 \nSupdt. of Police 119 66 53 \nAddl. Supdt. of Police 91 68 23 \nDy. Supdt. of Police 301 219 82 \nInspector of Police 1024 827 197 \nSub Inspector of Police 480 199 281 \nAsstt. Sub-Inspector of Police 207 191 16 \nHd. Constable 583 530 53 \nConstables 2119 1750 369 \nTotal 5000 3899 1101 \n \nOVER ALL VACANCY POSITION OF CBI \nDesignation of posts Sanctioned Strength Actual Strength Vacancy \nExecutive Ranks 5000 3899 1101 \nLaw Officers  370 198 172 \nTechnical Officers 162 97 65 \nMinisterial Staff 1672 1458 214 \nCanteen Posts 70 44 26 \nGrand Total 7274 5696 1578 \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_466": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS \n(DEPARTMENT OF PERSONNEL AND TRAINING) \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 466 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015) \n \nMATERNITY BENEFITS TO SURROGATE MOTHERS \n \n466. SHRIMATI VASANTHI M.: \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the Central and State Governments have no \npolicy of maternity benefit for surrogate mothers; and \n \n (b)  if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions and Minister \nof State in the Prime Minister’s Office. (DR. JITENDRA SINGH) \n(a) & (b):   Government of India considers grant of Maternity Leave to surrogate mothers on \ncase to case basis keeping in view the totality of the circumstances of each case.   Recently \nMaternity Leave was granted in a few cases. \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_467": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 467 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \nUDAIPUR-KHAJURAHO INTERCITY EXPRESS ROUTE \n \n†467.  SHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details  of number of applications received for curtailing the \nUdaipur-Khajuraho Intercity express route half from Mahoba and \nextending it upto Chitrakoot Dham; and \n \n(b) the action taken by the Railways on the above said applications?   \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a) & (b): Proposal contained in representations received regarding \ndetachment/attachment of half of the rake of 19665/19666 Udaipur -\nKhajuraho Intercity Express at Mahoba and extending it upto \nChitrakootdham Kar vi station has been examined and not found \nfeasible, at present, due to operational and resource constraints, \nincluding lack of adequate terminal/maintenance facility at \nChitrakootdham Karvi station.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_468": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 468 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT PROGRAMMES FOR UNEDUCATED PEOPLE \n \nQUESTION \n \n468.  SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n \n(a)  the details of skill development programmes in the country for the uneducated \npeople, course-wise, programme-wise; and \n \n \n(b) the details of the number of beneficiaries under these programmes during each \nof the last three years and the current year, State-wise, programme-wise, thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY) \n \n(a) to (b) There are over 70 skill development schemes across various sectors \nimplemented by over 20 Central Ministries/Departments to promote skilling  of all people \nincluding the uneducated, in the Country. Funds under these schemes are provided by \nthe concerned Ministries/Departments to the implementing agencies including States in \naccordance with the guidelines issued for the schemes. \n \nDetails of people  including uneducated trained under various skill development \nschemes/programmes of over 20 Central Ministries/Departments for the year 2012-13 \nto 2015-16 (upto June, 2015) are as under:- \n      (Figures in lakhs) \n \nYear \n \n \nNumber of persons trained \n2012-2013 51.88 \n2013-2014 76.37 \n2014-2015 76.12 \n2015-2016 (upto June, 2015) 09.73 \n  \n \n*******  \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_469": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.469 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 02, 2015 \n \nMEGA CITIES \n \nNo. 469  SHRI CHAND NATH:  \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is consid ering to develop some cities \nas mega cities on the lines of smart city; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any criteria has been fi xed for this purpose and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(d) whether some cities of Rajast han have also b een selected for \nthis purpose; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n \n(a): No, Madam. There is at present no proposal for developing some \ncities as Mega cities on the lines of Smart city.   \n \n(b)to(e):  Does not arise.   \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_470": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 470 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \nBG LINE BETWEEN LUMDING TO SILCHAR \n470. SHRI RADHESHYAM BISWAS: \n  Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has plans to start passenger trains in the newly \nconverted broad-gauge line between Lumding to Silchar within few months and \nif so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the steps taken for better connectivity of the peop le of Barak Valley of \nAssam, Mizoram, Manipur and Tripura with the rest of India; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to construct parallel track from Katihar \n(Bihar) to Dibrugarh (Assam) for fast connectivity of trains running without delay \nor halt for crossing in stations and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has any plan for electrification and getting electric  \ntrains for the NE States and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 470 BY SHRI RADHESHYAM BISWAS TO BE  ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 02.12.2015 REGARDING BG LINE BETWEEN LUMDING TO SILCHAR. \n \n(a) 55615/55616 Guwahati -Silchar Fast Passenger train via Lumding has been \nintroduced from 21.11.2015. \n \n(b) Barak valley has already been connected with the rest of the country by \nBroad Gauge.  Gauge Conversion of exist ing metre gauge lines of Badarpur -\nKumarghat-Agartala, Kathakal-Bhairabi and Arunachal –Jiribam have been taken \nup which will provide Broad Gauge connectivity to Tripura, Mizoram and \nManipur.  Further, to connect the capitals of Mizoram and Manipur, two new  line \nprojects viz Bhairabi-Sairang and Jiribam-Imphal have also been taken up. \n \n(c) On this route: \n(i) Barsoi-Raninagar is already a double line section. \n(ii) In Raninagar-Samuktala Road, doubling has been sanctioned. \n(iii) Samuktala-New Bongaigaon is already a double line section. \n(iv) Two independent lines exist between New Bongaigaon & Kamakhya. \n(v) Kamakhya-Digaru is already a double line section. \n(vi) In Digaru-Lumding: Doubling has been sanctioned. \n(vii) Presently, there is no proposal to doub le Katihar -Barsoi and Lumding -\nDibrugarh sections. However, on completion of Bogibeel bridge, a complete \nalternation route between Rangiya & Dibrugarh will be available. \n \n(d) & (e) With a view to provide faster and seamless rail connectivity to North \nEastern Region, electrification of Barauni -Katihar-Guwahati including Katihar -\nBarsoi (836 Route Kilometers (RKM) has been sanctioned at a cost of ₹821.53 \ncrores in which 223 RKM fal ls in the State of Assam.  The work has been \nentrusted to Central Organization for Railway Electrification (CORE) and Rail \nVikas Nigam Limited (RVNL) for execution. \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_471": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.471 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2ND December, 2015 \n \nLANDLINE BROADBAND SERVICES \n \n471.  SHRI A. ARUNMOZHITHEVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited is providing a minimum 2 Mbps speed to \nits landline broadband users at no extra cost; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the said move has triggered or may trigger a drop in data tariffs for \nhigher speed by private telecom operators; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e)  whether the BSNL is also considering to come out with more sops for its \ncustomers after the success of free roaming and free night call on landlines; and \n \n(f)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) &(b)  Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) has upgraded the bandwidth \nunder all ADSL (Asymmetric Digital Subscriber Line) Broadband plans of 512 kbps & 1 \nMbsp to 2 Mbps with Fair Usage Policy (FUP) w.e.f. 1.10.2015 without a ny additional \ncost to the customers. \n  \n(c) &(d)   No such specific consequent action by private telecom operators has \ncome to notice. \n \n(e) &(f)  BSNL, being a commercial organization, introduces appropriate schemes \nwithin the regulatory provisions from time to time for the benefits of its customer s and to \nincrease its market share.   \n \n***************** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_472": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.472 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 02, 2015 \nINSURANCE COVER FOR GOVERNMENT BUILDINGS  \n \nNo.472   SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to bring all the Government \nbuildings under insurance cover against natural calamities;   \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n (a) : No, Madam. \n(b): Does not arise. \n(c): In accordance to provisions contained in Rule 15 of Delegation of \nFinancial Power Rules and in CPWD Works Manual para 39.1(1), \nthe Government property both movable and immovable should \nnot normally be insured, without prior consent of Ministry of \nFinance. \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_473": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 473 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \nUNUSABLE COACHES AND WAGONS \n \n \n†473.  DR. RAVINDRA KUMAR RAY: \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of the passenger coaches and goods wagons in \nthe railways which are not in working condition since many \nyears, zone-wise; and  \n \n(b) the steps taken/likely to be taken by the Government for the \ndisposal/reuse or use for other purposes of the said \ncoaches/wagons?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n (SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a) & (b): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (b) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 473 BY DR. RAVINDRA KUMAR RAY TO \nBE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 02.12.2015 REGARDING \nUNUSABLE COACHES AND WAGONS.  \n \n(a) The number of the passenger coaches and wagons in the \nRailways which are not in working condition and awaiting \ncondemnation are as under: \nS.No. Railway Zone Coaches Wagons \n1.  North Eastern Railway 6 141 \n2.  Northern  Railway 9 0 \n3.  Northeast Frontier Railway 101* 40 \n4.  North Western Railway 6 5 \n5.  Eastern Railway 0 0 \n6.  Southern Railway 0 0 \n7.  Central Railway 0 0 \n8.  South Western Railway 0 0 \n9.  North Central Railway 0 0 \n10.  East Central Railway 0 0 \n11.  West Central Railway 0 0 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n*Out of 101 coaches, 96 numbers are surplus on account of gauge \nconversion. \n(b) Coaches and wagons are taken out of service if found overaged \nor damaged beyond economical repair in accidents, derailments etc. \nCertain Metre gauge coaches and wagons are taken out of service if \nthey become surplus owing to gauge conversion. These coaches  and \nwagons are either condemned, sold or sometimes retained for display \ndue to heritage value.                                                        