    "ls|LS16_S6_U_485": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 485 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \nPROPOSAL FOR TORI-SHIVPUR-KATHOTIA LINE \n \n†485.  SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Railways had proposed laying of Tori -Shivpur-Kathotia \nrailway line;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the estimated cost of the line and \nthe funds released for the same so far;   \n(c)   the current status of the said line; and  \n(d)   the time by which it is likely to be completed? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) to (d):  Yes, Madam. Tori-Shivpur-Kathautia new line project has been \ntaken up at an anticipated cost of `3596 crores and funds to the tune of \n`958 crores have been released by M/s Central Coalfields Limited for this \nproject upto March 2015. The project has been taken up in two phases \nas detailed below: \n(i) Tori-Shivpur (44 km) section at an anticipated cost of `1589 crore. In \nthis section, about 74% land has been acquired and earthwork, \nbridgeworks have been taken up. \n(ii) Shivpur-Kathautia(49 km) section at an anticipated cost of `2007 \ncrores. Alignment has been staked on the ground and land plans \nsubmitted to the State Government for land acquisition. \nThe timeline for completion of the project has not been fixed due to \nuncertainty in acquisition of land and forestry clearances. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_486": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.486 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \nAID TO COUNTRIES \n \n486.   SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \nDR. VIRENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether India has given aid to other nations in the last three \nyears for reconstruction, economic development and \nmitigation of environmental disasters; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, year and country-wise; and \n(c) the benefits accrued to these countries through these funds? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n (a) to (c) Information in respect of aid to countries has been \ncompiled and placed at Annexure. \n \n***** \n\n1 \n \nANNEXURE \nAID TO COUNTRIES \n \nFinancial \nYear \n \nCountry \n \nDetails of Aid Provided/benefits accrued to these countries through these funds Amount (Rs.) \n2012-13 Republic of Congo Cash grant – humanitarian assistance-accidental fire(USD 500,000) 2.601 Crores \nNiger Supply of 100 computers and printer towards capacity building 0.621 crores \nLiberia Supply of 25 buses 0.443 crores \nMali Grant for support to the African led International support Mission in Mali (USD 1 \nmillion) 5.408 crores \n Bhutan India provides broad-based multi sectoral development assistance to Bhutan.  \nOur developmental aid has contributed to socio -economic development and  further \nstrengthened our bilateral cooperation with Bhutan in diverse fields, including areas of \nhydropower, infrastructure and connectivity, health, agriculture and irrigation, rural and \ncommunity development, education and training defence and security cooperation. \nNon Plan  Plan \n1171.06 \ncrore \n1538 \ncrore \n Republic of \nDominica \nHumanitarian assistance after flash floods.  \nThe aid given by India has been helpful to the countries in tiding over the effects of \nnatural disasters, helped in capacity building in improvement of their infrastructure. \n \nUS $300,000 \n Saint Lucia Humanitarian assistance after flash floods.  \nThe aid given by India has been helpful to the countries in tiding over the effects of \nnatural disasters, helped in capacity building in improvement of their infrastructure. \nUS $500,000 \n Saint Vincent and \nthe Grenadines \nHumanitarian assistance for  floods.  \nThe aid given by India has been helpful to the countries in tiding over the effects of \nnatural disasters, helped in capacity building in improvement of their infrastructure. \n \nUS $500,000 \n Total aid for \nCapacity Building \nwhich includes aid \nDPA-II Division provided aid/loan/ advances and disaster relief in countries of the \nSouth.  ITEC also forms an important component in the aid which aims at skill \ndevelopment, transfer of technology and sharing experiences with the partner countries \nRs.21049.34 lakhs \n\n2 \n \nfor Disaster Relief \nof Rs.2729.63 lakh \nto Republic of \nCongo, Bangladesh, \nLibya, Fiji, \nPhilippines, \nMyanmar, Syria, \nComoros, \nSeychelles, \nGuatemala, yemen  \n Nepal  India provides broad-based multi sectoral development assistance to Nepal.  \n \nOur developmental aid has contributed to socio -economic development and further \nstrengthened our bilateral cooperation with Nepal in diverse fields, inc luding areas of \ninfrastructure and connectivity, health, agriculture and irrigation, rural and community \ndevelopment, education and training, defence and security cooperation, water resources \nincluding river embankments and hydropower. \n \n305.06 crores \n Sri Lanka GOI is committed to assisting the Government of Sri Lanka in resettlement and \nrehabilitation of Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) in Northern and Eastern Sri Lanka.  \nIn this regard, the Government has been implementing several developmental projec ts, \nparticularly in the areas of development of rail and port infrastructure, connectivity and \ntransportation, agriculture, education, public health, housing, vocational training and \neconomic revival and women’s empowerment, particularly war widoes.  Towar ds this \neffort Governement has been utilising funds. \n \n \nThe main objective of the developmental projects being implemented by India in the \nNorthern and Eastern Sri Lank s is to assist Government of Sri Lanka in its relief, \nresettlement, rehabilitation and r econstruction efforts, including to ensure welfare and \nwell-being of the Tamils affected  by protracted civil war \n \n248 crores \n \n\n3 \n \n Palestine -India’s Annual contribution to UNRWA \n \nPrimarily for provision of food and medicines to the displaces people in the refugee \ncamps under UNRWA’s various programmes \n \n-Budgetary Support \n \nPart of US$ 10 million pledge announced during the visit of Presidsent Abbas to New \nDelhi in November 2012. \n \n-Support for Building of two Schools \n \nAn MoU was signed during the visit of President Abbas to India in November 2012 to \nbuild two schools in Palestine with the support of GOI \n \nUS$ 1 million \n \n \n \n \n \nUS$ 1 million \n \n \n \nUS$180,000 \n \n Syria -Humanitarian assistance for Syria refugees \n \nProvided fortified biscuits for Syrian refugees through WHO. \n \nUS $0.33 million \n2013-14 Cape Verde Supply of computers and accessories 0.955 crores \nNigeria Training of 20 Personnel 0.097 crores \n Total aid for \nCapacity Building \nwhich includes aid \nfor Disaster Relief \nof Rs.1458.22 lakh \nto Yemen, marshal \nIslands, Philippines, \nMadagascar, Latin \nAmerican \nCountries, \nBangladesh, Libya. \nCambodia \nDPA-II Division provided aid/loan/ advances and disaster rel ief in countries of the \nSouth.  ITEC also forms an important component in the aid which aims at skill \ndevelopment, transfer of technology and sharing experiences with the partner countries \nRs.21684.06 lakh \n\n4 \n \n Ethiopia Support to establish Vocational Training Centre \n Rs. 5 Crores \n  \nBhutan \nIndia provides broad-based multi sectoral development assistance to Bhutan.  \nOur developmental aid has contributed to socio -economic development and further \nstrengthened our bilateral cooperation with Bhutan in diverse fields, including areas of \nhydropower, infrastructure and connectivity, health, agriculture and irrigation, rural and \ncommunity development, education and training defence and security cooperation. \n \nNon Plan \nRs.1339 \nCrores \n \nPlan \n \nRs. \n2589.27 \ncrores \n Jamaica Aid for flood lights at the Sabina Park Cricket Stadium. US $2.1million \n Guatemala Cash relief assistance after drought US $200,000 \n Suriname Aid for up-gradation of a technical institute  US $169,400 \n Fiji India has been supporting Fiji for various developmental activities, economic \ndevelopment and disaster relief. \n \nAid for various projects including Memorial to the “Syria” Shipwreck Victims, Supply \nof sewing machines, medical kits, ambulances, support for Fiji elections, hospital \nequipment, ambulances and support for health care education \n \nThe assistance was meant to support various developmental activities and relief & \nrehabilitation measures, and was appreciated by the Government of Fiji. \nUS$ 86,15,751 \n Marshall Islands \n(RMI) \nGovernment of  India has been providing aid and support to Government of Marshall \nIslands for various developmental activities, disaster relief etc. \n \nGrant-in-aid for hosting PIF Summit Meeting in September 2013. \nThe GOI assisted projects have generated a tremendous amo unt of goodwill in Marshall \nIslands. \n \n \n \n \n \nUS$ 162,833.10 \n Philippines India had provided the following relief supply in the immediate aftermath of Super \nTyphoon Haiyan in November 2013: \n(i) Medicine - 1.2 Tonne \n(ii) Blanket, Bedding equipment  \n(iii) Tentage  \n \n \n \n \n \n\n5 \n \n(iv)ready to eat meals, cooking vessels - 12 Tonnes \n \nWe also handed over an aid in January 2014 for disaster relief to the Philippines for the \nearthquake in Bohol \n \nIndian aid were given for disaster relief and to support reconstruction. \n \nUSD 100,000 \n Palau Disaster relief support due to Typhoon Haiyan in Palau of amount  \n \nLogistic support for the 45th Pacific Islands Forum and related meetings \n \nFor Purchase of mini-van for the 45th Pacific Islands Forum and related meetings \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated by all the Pacific Island countries as it was \nmeant for minor critical projects which were selected by respective countries. \n \nUS$ 100,000 \n \nUS$ 99,000 \n \nUS$ 68,610 \n Cook Islands  Assistance for Sponsorship of Cricket Tournament. \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated as it was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \n \nUS$ 10,000 \n Tuvalu Financial assistance for participation in Water Week 2013. \n \nMedical assistance to Princess Margaret hospital \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated by all the Pacific Island countries as it was \nmeant for minor critical projects which were selected by respective countries. \n \nUS$ 23,675 \n \nUS$ 417,333 \n Tonga Financial assistance for Tsunami Alert System \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated by all the Pacific Island countries as it was \nmeant for minor critical projects which were selected by respective countries. \n \nUS$ 300,000 \n\n6 \n \n Kiribati Aid for funding of Health equipment. \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated by all the Pacific Island countries as it was \nmeant for minor critical projects which were selected by respective countries. \nUS$ 258,515 \n Papua New Guinea Aid for Purchase of two ambulances for the Health Department of West New Britain. \n \n-for purchase of computer, Bed bunks \n \n-for Memorial built to honor Indian soldiers and prisoners of war \n \n-for Purchase of equipment for a Community Project Assistance Scheme in East Sepik \nProvince \n \n-for support for St. Charles Lwanga Secondary School \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated as it was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \nUS$ 1,67,686.21 \n \n \nUS$ 78,112 \n \n \nUS$ 20,967 \n \n \nUS$ 34,431 \n \n \nUS$ 88,225 \n Solomon islands Aid for Procurement of a solar power system and freezer compartment for Paranau Bay \nFisheries Project. \n \nAid for Disaster relief for Flash floods \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated as it was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \n \nUS$ 20,930 \n \n \nUS$ 50,000 \n Nepal India provides broad-based multi sectoral development assistance to Nepal.   \n \nOur developmental aid has contributed to socio -economic development and further \nstrengthened our bilateral cooperation with Nepal in diverse fields, including areas of \ninfrastructure and connectivity, health, agriculture and irrigation, rural and community \ndevelopment, education and training, defence and security cooperation, water resources \nincluding river embankments and hydropower. \n381.25 crores \n\n7 \n \n Sri Lanka GOI is committed to assisting the Government of Sri Lanka in resettlement and \nrehabilitation of Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) in Northern and Eastern Sri Lanka.  \nIn this regard, the Government has been implementing s everal developmental projects, \nparticularly in the areas of development of rail and port infrastructure, connectivity and \ntransportation, agriculture, education, public health, housing, vocational training and \neconomic revival and women’s empowerment, part icularly war widoes.  Towards this \neffort Governement has been utilising funds. \n \nThe main objective of the developmental projects being implemented by India in the \nNorthern and Eastern Sri Lank s is to assist Government of Sri Lanka in its relief, \nresettlement, rehabilitation and reconstruction efforts, including to ensure welfare and \nwell-being of the Tamils affected  by protracted civil war \n420.8 crores \n Palestine -India’s Annual contribution to UNRWA \n \nPrimarily for provision of food and medicines to the displaces people in the refugee \ncamps under UNRWA’s various programmes \n \n-Budgetary Support \n \nPart of US$ 10 million pledge announced during the visit of President Abbas to New \nDelhi in November 2012. \n \nUS$ 1 million \n \n \n \n \nUS$ 4 million \n Syria -Humanitarian assistance for Syrian refugees delivered through UN-SHARP \n \n \nUS $ 1 million \n Lebanon Humanitarian assistance for Syrian refugees in Lebanon US $5,00,000 \n Jordan Humanitarian assistance for Syrian refugees in Jordan US $ 5,00,000 \n2014-15 Liberia, Sierra \nLeone, Guinea \nHumanitarian assistance to Ebola affected countries (USD 50,000 each to three \ncountries) 0.910 crores \nLiberia Humanitarian assistance to Ebola affected countries (USD 5581) 0.034 crores \n Total aid for Capacity Building which includes aid for Disaster Relief of Rs.2474.35 lakhs to Nicaragua, \nSerbia, Bosnia Herzegovina, Philippines, Croatia, Syria, Nepal, Solomon Islands, Algeria, Burundi, Yemen, Rs.22503.63 lakhs \n\n8 \n \nMadagascar \n Burundi Support to establish Vocational Training Centre Rs. 5 Crores \n Rwanda Support to establish Vocational Training Centre Rs. 5 Crores \n Myanmar In August 2015, in response to Government of Myanmar’s specific request for \nemergency supplies of rice and medicine s by air to locations which were hard to reach \nfollowing heavy floods and landslides in Myanmar, the Government of India dispatched \nemergency relief supplies including rice, medicines and readymade noodles to Myanmar \nby Indian Air Force aircraft. This time ly supply of relief material by air was highly \nappreciated by the people and Government of Myanmar \n(In the last three years, we followed on our development cooperation projects which \nwere committed in the past.  ) \n \n Bhutan India provides broad-based multi sectoral development assistance to Bhutan.  \nOur developmental aid has contributed to socio -economic development and further \nstrengthened our bilateral cooperation with Bhutan in diverse fields, including areas of \nhydropower, infrastructure and connectivity, health, agriculture and irrigation, rural and \ncommunity development, education and training defence and security cooperation. \n \n(During the current year Rs. 6160.20 crores have been allocated) \nNon Plan \n \n1146.59  \ncrores \nPlan \n \n3371.82 \ncrores \n \n Republic of \nDominica \nCash relief assistance after tropical storm Erica US $200,000 \n Guatemala For installation of solar traffic lights Rs.70 lakhs \n Guyana Cash assistance for election process US $50,000 \n Suriname Aid for reorganisation of a mortuary at S’lands Hospital in Paramaribo US $156,760 \n Fiji India has been supporting Fiji for various developmental activities, economic \ndevelopment and disaster relief \n \nAid for hospital equipment for Colonial War Memorial Hospital, and \nsupport for Fiji elections. \n \nAid  for supply of indelible ink for Fiji elections \n \nThe assistance was meant to support various developmental activities and relief & \nUS$ 4,49,806 \n \n \n \n \nRs. 68,81,600/- \n\n9 \n \nrehabilitation measures, and was appreciated by the Government of Fiji. \n \n(During the current year Rs.2,81,10,000/ - have been allocated for supply of 5,000 \nAkash/UbiSlate Tablets for Fiji School Children) \n Vanuatu Cash assistance to Vanuatu after wide spread damage and destruction caused by \ncyclones in the country in 2015. An amount was given to Vanuatu subsequent to \ncyclones PAM in March 2015 \n \nThe cash assistance was meant to support relief and rehabilitation measures, and was \nappreciated by the Government of Vanuatu. \nUS$ 250,000 \n Timor Leste  Aid for funding the production and supply of a fully equipped Ambulance unit to the \nGovernment of Timor Leste. The Ambulance unit is expected to be delivered to the \nGovernment of Timor Leste in December 2015. \n \nThe Timorese Government which is emphasizing on improving its health management \nsystem had requested for the supply of an Ambulance unit. Thus it is expected to benefit \nthe Timorese people and generate goodwill for the Government of India. \nUS$ 70,620 \n Combodia Government of India has been providing assistance in form of grant -in-aid, concessional \nloans to Cambodia for undertaking various developmental and construction projects, as \nwell as for the restoration and conservation of Angkor Wat and Ta-Prohm temples.  \n \nFinancial assistance for water development project in Siem Reap, the project approved in \n2014 and currently undergoing. \n \nFinancial assistance for water development project Kampong Speu, the project approved \nin 2014 and currently undergoing \n \nThe GOI assisted projects have generated a tremendous amount of goodwill in \nCambodia. \n \n(The Previous assistance is as follows : \n2004 – 2015 \n \n \n \n \n \nUS $ 1.15 million \n \n \n \nUS$ 1.8 million \n \n \n \n \n \n\n10 \n \nRs.34.16 Crores (Phase I & II) for Restoration and Preservation of Ta -Prohm Temple. \nThe project was commenced in 2004 pursua nt to an MOU that was signed in 2002 \nbetween the Govt. of India and Royal Government of Cambodia. Phase I and Phase II of \nthe project completed on 31 July 2015. \n \n2010-2015 \nUS$ 246,000 for Redevelopment of India -Cambodia Friendship School Project in \nKampong Cham province \nDuring the visit of President of India to Cambodia in September 2010, a grant -in-aid of \nUS$ 246,894 for the re -development of Cambodia -India Friendship School was \nannounced. The project has been completed successfully and handed over to the \nCambodian side during the visit of Vice President of India in September 2015.) \n \n \n \n \n Marshall Islands Government of India has been providing aid and support to Government of Marshall \nIslands for various developmental activities, disaster relief etc. \n \nGrant-in-aid to fund RMI's first National Export Strategy. \n \nThe GOI assisted projects have generated a tremendous amount of goodwill in Marshall \nIslands. \n \n(During the current year US $150,000 Special grant for organizing 2 nd Annual Pacific \nIsland Leaders’Summit in Majuro, RMI.) \n \n \n \nUS$ 100,570 \n Federated States of \nMicronesia (FSM) \nFor implementation of project FSM Integrated Agricultural census. \n \nThe FSM project seeks to generate baseline data on structure of agriculture and fishing \nin FSM, which will provide useful inputs for policy makers. \nUS$ 2,00,000 \n Cook Islands For 10 Projects proposal for various developmental activities \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated   as it was meant for minor critical  projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \nUS$ 1,24,332 \n Tuvalu Aid for purchasing of ambulances. \n \nUS$ 43,579 \n \n\n11 \n \nFinancial assistance for purchase of medical equipment for princess Margaret hospital, \nFunafuti \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated as it  was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \nUS$ 447,865 \n Papua New Guinea Aid for Solar Electrification in Jubilee Institute \n \n-for support for St. Charles Lwanga Secondary School \n \nAssistance from India i s much appreciated as it was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \n \n(During the current year US $51,654 have been allocated for purchase of vehicle for \nUniversity of PNG Clinic) \nUS$ 160,000 \n \n         US$ 88,225 \n Solomon islands Aid for Disaster relief for Flash floods. \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated as it was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \n \n \nUS$ 50,000 \n Nauru Aid for welfare of expatriates working for the Government. \n \nAssistance from India is much appreciated as it was meant for minor critical projects \nwhich were selected by respective countries. \n \n(During the current year US$4,50,175 have been allocated for construction of Sea wall \nfor protection against rising sea.) \n \nUS$ 2,44,112 \n Nepal  India provides broad -based multi sectoral development assistance to Nepal. When a \ndevastating earthquake hit Nepal in April 2015, India was the first responder with its \nlargest ever disaster relief operation called ‘Op eration Maitri.’ Our monetized assistance \nin the relief phase was nearly Rs. 400 crores. For the long term rehabilitation phase, \n300 crores \n\n12 \n \nIndia announced its commitment of US$ 1 billion, one -fourth of which would be as \ngrant.  \nOur developmental aid has contributed to socio -economic development and further \nstrengthened our bilateral cooperation with Nepal in diverse fields, including areas of \ninfrastructure and connectivity, health, agriculture and irrigation, rural and community \ndevelopment, education and training, defence and security cooperation, water resources \nincluding river embankments and hydropower. \n \n(During the current financial year an aid worth Rs.420 crores has been provided) \n \n Sri Lanka GOI is committed to assisting the Government of Sri Lanka in resettlement and \nrehabilitation of Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) in Northern and Eastern Sri Lanka.  \nIn this regard, the Government has been implementing several developmental projects, \nparticularly in the areas of development of rail and port infrastructure, connectivity and \ntransportation, agriculture, education, public health, housing, vocational training and \neconomic revival and women’s empowerment, particularly war widoes.  Towards this  \neffort Governement has been utilising funds. \n \nThe main objective of the developmental projects being implemented by India in the \nNorthern and Eastern Sri Lank s is to assist Government of Sri Lanka in its relief, \nresettlement, rehabilitation and reconstru ction efforts, including to ensure welfare and \nwell-being of the Tamils affected  by protracted civil war. \n \n(During current year Rs. 500 crores have been allocated) \n \nRs. 500 crores \n Palestine -India’s Annual contribution to UNRWA \n \nPrimarily for provision of food and medicines to the displaces people in the refugee \ncamps under UNRWA’s various programmes \n \n-Budgetary Support \n \nUS$ 1 million \n \n \n \n \nUS$ 5 million \n \n\n13 \n \nPart of US$ 10 million pledge announced during the visit of Presidsent Abbas to New \nDelhi in November 2012. \n \n-Support for Building of two Schools \n \nAn MoU was signed during the visit of President Abbas to India in November 2012 to \nbuild two schools in Palestine with the support of GOI \n \n \n \n \n \nUS$ 46000 \n Syria -Humanitarian assistance for Syrian refugees delivered through UN-SHARP \n \n \nUS $ 1 million \n Lebanon Humanitarian assistance for Syrian refugees in Lebanon US $ 5,00,000 \n Jordan Humanitarian assistance for Syrian refugees in Jordan US $ 5,00,000 \n Bosnia & \nHerzegovina \n(Sarajevo) \nGOI provided aid following massive floods causing immense destruction to lives and \nproperty.  The amount has been contributed towards disaster/flood relief. \nUS$1,00,000 \n Serbia (Belgrade) During last three years, GOI extended financial assistance to Serbi a as immediate relief \nfrom the disastrous effects of the catastrophic floods. \nUS$1,00,000 \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_487": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.487 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.12.2015 \n \nINDO-CHINA MEET ON BORDER ISSUE \n  \n487.  SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Ind ian and Chinese officials met recently for the \nlatest round of talks under the mechanism set up for both \ncountries to consult and coordinate on border related issues; \nand \n \n(b)  if so, the deliberations made in the said meeting? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) & (b) The latest meeting of the Working Mechanism for \nConsultation and Coordination on India -China Boundary Affairs was \nheld in Beijing on 8 October 2015. Both sides discussed variou s \nissues pertaining to the maintenance of peace and tranquillity in the \nborder areas. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_488": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION  \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 488 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 02, 2015 \n \nHOUSING AND SLUM DEVELOPMENT \n \nNo. 488 \n  \nSHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to implement the Integrated \nHousing and Slum Development Programme in Madhya Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, area-wise; \n(c) the details of work done by the Government for slum development during \nthe last three years in the State; and  \n(d) the amount spent for the said work? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n \n(a & b): Under Integrated Housing and Slum Development Programme \n(IHSDP), 50 projects with a total project cost of Rs.235.34 crore involving Rs. \n160.31 crore of central share for construction of 13635 dwelling units (DUs) \nhave been approved in  Madhya Pradesh. Details of the projects, area-wise, \nare at Annexure-I. \n \n(c) & (d): The details of work done by the Government for slum development \nin the State of Madhya Pradesh and amount spent during the last three years \nunder BSUP and IHSDP components of JNNURM as well as under Rajiv Awas \nYojana (RAY) are at Annexure-II. \n \n*****\n \n\nScheme No.\tof\t\nProjects\nProject\nCost\nCentral\t\nShare\nCentral\t\nShare\t\nReleased\nDwelling\t\nUnits\t\nSanctioned\nConstruction\t\nCompleted\nDUs\tIn‐\t\nProgress\nFunds\t\tReleased\t\t\nduring\tLast\t\nthree\tyears\tand\t\nCurrent\tYear\t\nConstruction\t\ncompleted\t\nDuring\tLast\t\nthree\tyears\tand\t\nCurrent\tYear\t\nBSUP 21 473.44    230.75    268.56      24728 21736 2992 37.02             12655\nIHSDP 50 235.34    160.31    197.31      13635 11345 2290 60.55             9802\nRAY 8 444.32     229.65    190.70      8123 -             2434 58.77             -    